WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:08.291
People have always looked up at the moon with wonder.

2
00:00:09.352 --> 00:00:15.374
Ancient people worshipped the moon as a goddess, and many believed that she was responsible for the creation

3
00:00:15.414 --> 00:00:15.914
of time.

4
00:00:17.015 --> 00:00:22.817
The first calendars were based on the phases of the moon, which change consistently to complete a full

5
00:00:22.897 --> 00:00:24.718
cycle every 28 days.

6
00:00:25.818 --> 00:00:32.409
In the moon's bright features, people have imagined a human face, a rabbit, and even a round piece

7
00:00:32.449 --> 00:00:32.969
of cheese.

8
00:00:34.029 --> 00:00:40.411
It was not until the twentieth century, however, that the United States and the Soviet Union began to

9
00:00:40.451 --> 00:00:44.712
develop technology that would allow humans to visit the alluring white sphere.

10
00:00:45.892 --> 00:00:53.554
Both countries were set on conquering outer space; both wanted to prove their scientific superiority and their military

11
00:00:53.614 --> 00:00:59.856
strength. This "space race," as it became known, served to fuel people's interest in the moon.

12
00:01:01.252 --> 00:01:08.158
The first major hurdle to space travel was overcome by the Soviet Union in 1957 with the launch

13
00:01:08.198 --> 00:01:09.979
of the first artificial satellite.

14
00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:15.283
Sputnik, as it was called, was catapulted into orbit by a rocket.

15
00:01:16.184 --> 00:01:22.749
It weighed about 83 kilograms and looked like a giant ball with several antennas that protruded out like

16
00:01:22.809 --> 00:01:24.211
whiskers on a cat's nose.

17
00:01:25.372 --> 00:01:32.753
It also had two radio transmitters attached to its spherical body that sent out signals, helping Soviet scientists

18
00:01:32.793 --> 00:01:34.735
learn more about the density of space.

19
00:01:35.796 --> 00:01:42.342
After completing its task, Sputnik was brought back toward the earth, but was destroyed upon reentry into the

20
00:01:42.422 --> 00:01:48.928
atmosphere. Despite the loss of Sputnik, the experiment was considered a great success.

21
00:01:49.929 --> 00:01:56.915
As a result, the Soviets attempted several more satellite launches, including one that took a dog, named Laika,

22
00:01:57.075 --> 00:01:57.856
up into space.

23
00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:02.742
Laika became the first living creature from Earth to travel into orbit.

24
00:02:04.002 --> 00:02:11.225
The first human to leave Earth's atmosphere was also part of the Soviet team, Colonel Yuri Gagarin.

25
00:02:11.385 --> 00:02:16.908
In 1961, Gagarin was chosen to command a space capsule called Vostok 1.

26
00:02:17.908 --> 00:02:22.010
It blasted off from a secret launch pad located in Soviet Kazakhstan.

27
00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:26.412
It traveled a complete circle around the earth in just 108 minutes.

28
00:02:27.856 --> 00:02:29.897
Then, it returned to the ground over Russia.

29
00:02:30.797 --> 00:02:35.539
Before it landed, Gagarin ejected from the capsule and parachuted to safety.

30
00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:41.982
After this adventurous ride, many Soviets started to call Gagarin "the Columbus of the Cosmos."

31
00:02:43.203 --> 00:02:49.045
Meanwhile, the United States was trying desperately to match the success of the Soviet space program.

32
00:02:50.145 --> 00:02:56.048
Unfortunately, their first attempt to launch a satellite had ended in an explosion on the launch pad.

33
00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:03.862
But this did not deter the Americans, and soon after Gagarin's historic flight, they launched their own manned

34
00:03:03.922 --> 00:03:07.963
mission, Freedom 7, carrying Alan Shepard into space.

35
00:03:09.204 --> 00:03:14.805
Shepard, who had been a navy test pilot, earned the distinction of being the first astronaut to return

36
00:03:14.885 --> 00:03:18.106
safely with his ship without having to use a parachute.

37
00:03:19.046 --> 00:03:24.628
This inspired a second mission, Friendship 7, piloted by astronaut John Glenn.

38
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:30.843
With this limited amount of manned space experience, American President John F.

39
00:03:30.883 --> 00:03:38.007
Kennedy announced that his country would embark on a new space program managed by their aeronautics agency, NASA.

40
00:03:38.847 --> 00:03:44.190
He made a promise to the American public that, before the end of the decade, the country would

41
00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:48.072
send an astronaut to the moon and return him to the earth unharmed.

42
00:03:49.113 --> 00:03:54.816
This program was named Apollo after the Greek god of the sun, and work began immediately.

43
00:03:55.888 --> 00:04:02.132
The initial stage was to determine how NASA engineers should build a space shuttle that would reach the

44
00:04:02.172 --> 00:04:06.054
moon while keeping costs low and minimizing risk to human life.

45
00:04:06.974 --> 00:04:14.679
After much debate, they decided to focus on constructing an Apollo spacecraft that consisted of four main sections.

46
00:04:15.579 --> 00:04:21.702
The Command Module was the part where the astronauts would sit when the spacecraft was launched and where

47
00:04:21.743 --> 00:04:24.084
they would spend most of their time while on board.

48
00:04:25.372 --> 00:04:30.777
The Service Module contained all of their equipment, such as oxygen tanks and the engine.

49
00:04:31.597 --> 00:04:36.982
The Lunar Module was a small part of the ship that could detach from the main spacecraft.

50
00:04:37.842 --> 00:04:40.505
It was the section that would actually land on the moon.

51
00:04:41.405 --> 00:04:47.090
The final section, the Launch Escape Tower, would be used during the launch and would be discarded shortly

52
00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:47.971
after takeoff.

53
00:04:49.132 --> 00:04:55.858
Altogether, the four sections fit together to look like a long, thin rocket with a wide, round tailpipe.

54
00:04:56.738 --> 00:05:02.083
For several years, engineers worked hard to design a fully functioning Apollo spacecraft.

55
00:05:03.204 --> 00:05:06.227
In the beginning, the program suffered some setbacks.

56
00:05:07.368 --> 00:05:13.653
During a test of Apollo 1, an explosion resulted in a fire that killed three astronauts on the

57
00:05:13.693 --> 00:05:14.354
launch pad.

58
00:05:15.275 --> 00:05:22.077
This was devastating to the program, so engineers decided to focus on testing unmanned flights until they were

59
00:05:22.177 --> 00:05:24.679
certain that no more astronauts would be harmed.

60
00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:33.186
After six major tests, in 1968, the Americans were ready to allow astronauts to pilot spacecraft again.

61
00:05:34.347 --> 00:05:41.613
Human-piloted Apollo 7 circled the moon in October of that year, and three more manned missions were launched

62
00:05:41.633 --> 00:05:43.935
to test various sections of the spacecraft.

63
00:05:44.836 --> 00:05:49.540
Then, in 1969, NASA announced that it was prepared to reach the moon.

64
00:05:50.656 --> 00:05:56.799
Commander Neil Armstrong, Michael Collins, and Buzz Aldrin were chosen for the mission, Apollo 11.

65
00:05:57.779 --> 00:06:04.642
Like Alan Shepard, Armstrong was a navy test pilot with a lot of experience flying experimental airplanes.

66
00:06:05.542 --> 00:06:11.045
He had previously worked on other American space missions and was known to cope well with periods of

67
00:06:11.105 --> 00:06:12.085
extreme stress.

68
00:06:13.246 --> 00:06:20.805
Armstrong would command the crew, Collins would pilot the Command Module (called Columbia), and Aldrin would be responsible

69
00:06:20.845 --> 00:06:23.645
for piloting the Lunar Module (called the Eagle).

70
00:06:24.626 --> 00:06:32.508
Apollo 11 was launched from the Kennedy Space Center in Florida at 1:32 pm on July 16, 1969.

71
00:06:33.548 --> 00:06:36.749
After four days of travel, it began orbiting the moon.

72
00:06:37.809 --> 00:06:44.391
When the spacecraft was in position, Aldrin and Armstrong climbed into the Eagle and separated from Columbia.

73
00:06:44.411 --> 00:06:49.149
"The Eagle has wings," Armstrong reported to ground control.

74
00:06:50.089 --> 00:06:54.750
The descent to the moon took almost three hours and the landing was full of tension.

75
00:06:55.630 --> 00:07:01.072
The Eagle was coming in too fast, and the flight controllers back on Earth thought the astronauts would

76
00:07:01.112 --> 00:07:01.872
have to abort.

77
00:07:02.932 --> 00:07:05.773
Armstrong saw that they were heading for a field of boulders.

78
00:07:06.773 --> 00:07:12.235
He took manual control of the craft and began skimming across the surface to find a better spot.

79
00:07:13.435 --> 00:07:19.790
Moments later, with only thirty seconds of fuel remaining, the Eagle's footpads touched the moon's surface in the

80
00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:20.910
Sea of Tranquility.

81
00:07:21.851 --> 00:07:28.594
From inside the Eagle, Buzz Aldrin confirmed the successful touchdown with the words, "Tranquility base.

82
00:07:29.014 --> 00:07:30.235
The Eagle has landed."

83
00:07:31.176 --> 00:07:36.839
After several hours, Armstrong crawled out of the hatch and climbed down the outside ladder.

84
00:07:37.739 --> 00:07:43.262
From the bottom rung, he jumped the last three feet down to the moon and declared: "That's one

85
00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:46.844
small step for man, one giant leap for mankind."

86
00:07:48.052 --> 00:07:52.796
With these words, Neil Armstrong became the first person to walk on the moon.

87
00:07:53.876 --> 00:08:00.021
First, Armstrong took photographs and soil samples in case the mission had to be aborted suddenly.

88
00:08:00.841 --> 00:08:07.146
Then, he and Aldrin, who had climbed down after him, jumped across the landscape and planted the American

89
00:08:07.226 --> 00:08:14.451
flag. They also unveiled a plaque that read, "Here men from the planet Earth first set foot upon

90
00:08:14.491 --> 00:08:14.852
the Moon.

91
00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.041
July 1969 AD.

92
00:08:18.961 --> 00:08:21.022
We came in peace for all mankind."

93
00:08:21.883 --> 00:08:28.065
When Apollo 11 returned safely to Earth on July 24, the astronauts were welcomed as heroes.

94
00:08:29.266 --> 00:08:33.848
Kennedy's promise had been fulfilled and NASA had achieved a miraculous feat.

95
00:08:34.908 --> 00:08:39.530
Ancient people probably never imagined that a human would ever walk on the moon.

96
00:08:40.291 --> 00:08:47.201
But, Apollo 11 turned distant dreams into reality, bringing the ancient moon goddess firmly within grasp.
